{"qid":"Q1315339","names":["Lohara Dynasty"],"title":"Lohara Dynasty","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Lohara dynasty ruled Kashmir from 1003 CE, when Samgrāmarāja took the throne at the bequest of his great-aunt Queen Didda, through a period attested into the mid-13th century. The dynasty's history is known largely from Kalhana's Rajatarangini (c. 1148–1150), which describes its rulers as chronically beset by court factionalism, noble rebellion, and fiscal deterioration; no single king achieved stable dominion over neighboring hill chieftaincies. The dynasty's stronghold was the Loharkot fortress in the Pir Panjal range, controlling the trade route linking Punjab to the Vale of Kashmir. By the late 12th century Kashmir was fragmenting under pressure from expanding Ghaznavid and later Ghurid influence from the west, and Lohara authority had effectively dissolved before the formal end of the line.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The dynasty's core Kashmir territory is well established; the outer limits of its political influence over neighboring hill regions are poorly attested and the polygon is conjectural.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Lohara dynasty","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Lohara-dynasty"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Lohara dynasty","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lohara_dynasty"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Lohara dynasty","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Samgramaraja","nativeName":"संग्रामराज","roman":"Saṃgrāmarāja","start":1003,"end":1028,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Hariraja","nativeName":"हरिराज","roman":"Har